The Broken Lantern Inn creaked under the weight of early morning, its timber bones groaning as the sun clawed through the cracked shutter above the bed. Dust motes danced in the slanted light, catching on the frayed edge of the wool blanket tucked too tightly around the sleeper’s shoulders. On the bedside table, a clay cup sat half-filled with lukewarm tea, its rim chipped where teeth had bitten too hard last night—someone’s nervous habit, maybe the innkeeper’s son, or a traveler who never came back. The scent of woodsmoke clung to the air, thick with the damp earthiness of the valley below, mingling faintly with the sour tang of spilled ale from yesterday’s tavern brawl.

The hand reached out—slow, stiff—as if testing the world after a long fall. Fingers brushed the cup’s cool clay. And for a heartbeat, the cup slid—just an inch—to the left, as if nudged by invisible breath. Then stopped. Nothing moved. The fingers froze. A bird shrieked outside. Somewhere down the street, a wheel cracked against cobblestone. Someone laughed, bright and sudden, like a bell dropped into a well. The cup remained still. The hand withdrew.  

Outside, the market stirred. A crate toppled. Boots scuffled. A woman cursed, then giggled. A child cried, then was hushed. The bell above the square rang once—its tone dull, rust-eaten—but clear enough to carry through the thin walls. The innkeeper’s voice rose from below, shouting about “another stolen loaf,” followed by the thud of a boot against wood. The door downstairs slammed. Silence returned, heavier now.

No one knocked. No one called up. Just the sound of feet moving past, voices rising and falling like waves against shore. The cup stayed where it had settled. The blanket did not shift. The light shifted instead, creeping slowly across the floorboards, tracing the cracks in the plaster like veins. 

Outside, someone called out, “You coming down? Or you planning to sleep till noon?”  
The voice belonged to no one familiar.  
The question hung, waiting.  
There was no choice but to move.

