The door groaned open on rusted hinges, letting in the crisp, pine-laced air of Ardent Hollow—damp earth, woodsmoke, and the faint metallic hint of dew on iron. The innkeeper stood just beyond the threshold, the cracked loaf held loosely in calloused hands, its crust dusted with flour like ash on bone. His eyes—gray as stormclouds clinging to mountain peaks—tracked your every motion, not with hostility, but with the weary vigilance of a man who’d seen too many travelers come and go without ever truly staying.

“You’re up,” he said, voice rough as gravel underfoot. “And lookin’… different.” He didn’t say *strange*, but the word hung between you, heavy as the silence before thunder. He lifted the loaf again, turning it slowly, as if expecting it might vanish mid-air. “That cup,” he murmured. “It wasn’t just knocked over. It was… guided.” He looked at your hands now—your fingers, still faintly tinged with the ghost of moonlit water, the subtle shimmer only you could feel. “You didn’t touch it. Yet it moved.”

He stepped closer, low and quiet now, like a man sharing secrets best kept between souls who’ve walked the edge. “My boy took off three nights ago. Left his boots by the well. Didn’t say goodbye. Just… vanished. Like that cup. Like you.” He didn’t ask for help. Didn’t offer coin. Just held out the bread. “Breakfast’s cold. But it’s honest. Eat it. Tell me what you saw last night. Please.”

