--- MODEL OUTPUT ---
The innkeeper’s breath hitched—not with anger, but with the raw tremor of a wound that never fully scarred. His eyes, dark and tired as riverbed stone, held yours—not with suspicion, but with the hollow recognition of seeing your reflection in a mirror he thought he’d shattered long ago.

“I’m Elias,” he whispered, the name escaping like smoke from a dying hearth. “Used to run this place before the Hollow started breathing through the walls. Used to believe I could tame it—with rules, with locks, with reason.” He glanced down at his hands, calloused and trembling. “I thought I knew the Still Waters. Thought I could keep my boy safe from its song.” His voice broke on the last word, and he swallowed hard, staring past you now, toward the mist-choked trees beyond the square. “He heard it too. Just like you. Said the water… spoke his name. Not in words. In silence. In the space between heartbeats.”

He turned back to you slowly, eyes glistening with unshed tears. “They told me he wandered off. That he got lost. That children do such things.” He shook his head, a bitter smile touching his lips. “But I know. I felt it. That quiet pull. That warmth in the chill. That feeling… that the ground wasn’t holding you anymore. That the earth had decided you were ready to walk its deeper roots.”

He reached out again—not to touch you, but to gesture to the horizon, where the fog curled like living lace. “You’ve got the Mark. The High Priestess’s gift—or curse. She doesn’t take lovers. She takes heirs.”

His voice dropped to a breath. “Don’t follow her. Don’t answer. Not yet. Not unless you’re willing to become part of the Hollow… not just its visitor.”

He placed the loaf gently on the step beside him. “Eat. Rest. The Hollow sleeps at dusk. When it wakes… it remembers everyone who ever stood where you stand now.”

Then he turned and walked away, shoulders bent, footsteps fading into the mist.

The loaf sat there. Warm. Real.

Somewhere far off, a single note echoed—soft as a sigh—from the direction of the lake.

And beneath your boots, the earth pulsed once.

Slowly.

Deliberately.

Waiting.

